## Why This Is Asked

Tests involving asynchronous exports and email notifications are common in SaaS applications. This question evaluates practical familiarity with end-to-end testing flows, mocking external services, and ensuring idempotency under rapid repeated actions.

## Key Concepts

- Asynchronous job orchestration and eventual consistency
- Mocking external services (export service and email delivery)
- Test data isolation and seeding
- Idempotency and race condition handling

## Code Example

```javascript
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('data export flow is idempotent', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/dashboard');

  await seedTestData({ userId: 'user-123', dataset: [...] });

  await page.fill('#date-from', '2024-01-01');
  await page.fill('#date-to', '2024-01-31');
  await page.selectOption('#status', 'completed');

  await page.click('#export-btn');

  await page.route('**/api/export', async (route) => {
    await route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: { jobId: 'job-123', status: 'completed' } });
  });

  await page.route('**/api/email/send', async (route) => {
    await route.fulfill({ status: 200 });
  });

  await expect(page.locator('#download-link')).toBeVisible({ timeout: 10000 });

  const emailRequests = [];
  await page.on('request', (req) => {
    if (req.url().includes('/api/email/send')) {
      emailRequests.push(req);
    }
  });

  await page.click('#export-btn');
  await page.waitForTimeout(500);

  expect(emailRequests).toHaveLength(1);
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests at the beginner level often miss realistic offline scenarios and deterministic retries. This question probes practical E2E thinking: isolation, network control, and idempotent submission.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end orchestration of multi-step flows
- Auto-save timing and state persistence (e.g., localStorage/IndexedDB)
- Offline/online transition handling and reconciling state
- Mocking external APIs (identity) for determinism
- Ensuring single, idempotent final submit

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright sketch (context-level offline, data isolation, mocked API)
const context = await browser.newContext({ storageState: 'test-user.json' });
const page = await context.newPage();
await context.setOffline(true);
await page.goto('https://finapp/onboarding');
// perform step interactions, wait for auto-save marker, then go online
await context.setOffline(false);
// verify single submit
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to mobile vs desktop layouts?
- How would you validate that auto-save writes to the correct storage (localStorage vs IndexedDB) across browsers?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical E2E testing skills for beginners: data seeding, mocking remote services (feature flags), validating UI variants, and ensuring idempotent events within a fintech onboarding flow.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and remote config
- Test data seeding and isolation
- API interception/mocking
- Idempotent event verification
- Variant-driven UI testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Cypress skeleton for onboarding with feature flag
describe('Onboarding feature flag', () => {
  const userId = 'test-user-001';
  beforeEach(() => {
    cy.request('POST', '/test/setup-user', { userId });
  });

  it('Variant A renders steps A1/A2', () => {
    cy.intercept('/api/flags', { variant: 'A' }).as('flagA');
    cy.visit('/onboarding');
    cy.wait('@flagA');
    cy.get('.step1').should('be.visible');
  });

  it('Idempotent event when flag toggled', () => {
    cy.intercept('/api/flags', { variant: 'A' }).as('flagA');
    cy.visit('/onboarding');
    // complete some steps
    cy.reload(); // simulate flag refresh
    cy.intercept('/api/flags', { variant: 'B' }).as('flagB');
    cy.wait('@flagB');
    cy.get('@onlyOnboardingEvent').should('have.length', 1);
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to more than two variants and ensure coverage of each path?
- How would you integrate this with CI to catch flakey flag-based flows?

